All,

Full exploitation of the "Sewer Product Map" presented in Blurt 70 is aided=
 by articulation and comprehension of Harco's business strategy also discus=
sed at the sales meeting in the Sewer segment.  Comprehension of our strate=
gy can aid effective and efficient sales efforts and reduce frustration.

What is Harco's business strategy?  It is a "Differentiation" strategy.  Th=
e only other strategy available is "Low Cost".

What does "Differentiation" mean?  Differentiation refers to a conscious de=
cision to offer a product that requires a higher cost to make in order to d=
eliver a higher performance than competing substitutes.  A higher price is =
required and deserved provided a customer perceives and values the differen=
tiation.  The pursuit of a differentiation strategy requires a conscious un=
derstanding it limits the size of the profitable market that is addressable=
.  Not all will value the differentiation.  Efforts to grow share beyond th=
is space will by definition require reduced margins and will threaten the m=
argins of the profitable base.

Being simplistic, the essence of most of Harco's differentiations is a trad=
eoff that has one paying more in initial material cost in order to achieve =
a lower life time system cost.

Building the bridge between what our products can do and the perception req=
uired of a customer to accomplish profitable sales is the task of the Harco=
 Sales Team.

A differentiation strategy does not mean one is oblivious to market pricing=
 or that pricing is inflexible.

A factory sales team of the configuration and size (relative to sales) of H=
arco's is an added cost required to signal our value proposition.  A compan=
y pursuing a low cost strategy will have a lesser investment in the sales o=
rganization and effort.  A low cost strategy delivers a much simpler messag=
e, "we're cheaper".  This is almost never the case for Harco.

Harco's product offering is augmented by a "service" offering.  This servic=
e offering is a challenge to maintain as a differentiation (relative to com=
petitors) and thus to warrant a price premium in its own right.

The components of Harco's service offering are: commitment to inventory, to=
 have it to ship same or next day, distributed warehousing to reduce shippi=
ng times, product quality (different from product performance), flexibility=
 in price & terms, responsiveness to problems, return policy, product and a=
pplication knowledge, commercial and market knowledge, breadth of product l=
ines, and so on.

A big part of delivering this service offering and seeing to it that the se=
rvice is appreciated is the Harco Sales Team.  A differentiating element of=
 Harco's service offering is a factory sales team.

Augmenting our product offering with service can extend the life of product=
 lines (while maintaining acceptable margins) as their differentiation fade=
s in response to competitor changes.  Volume will decline in this phase of =
a product life cycle by definition.  It is important to understand that dif=
ferentiated product lines have a life cycle.  In time this differentiation =
can fade and the product can become commoditized.  At which point one has t=
o jump to a low cost structure or abandon the product line.

An example of this at Harco is the Ductile Iron Compact Mechanical Joint Fi=
tting.  Harco invented this product.  It's primary differentiation was the =
use of ductile iron instead of cast iron and the reduction of thicknesses p=
ermitted by the better mechanical properties resulting in a stronger, light=
er, and cheaper fitting than the competing cast iron fittings.  In time the=
se competitors (who had their own foundries) copied the product.  Their low=
er cost basis resulted in lower market pricing.  Faced with the need to inv=
est in foundries to reduce cost Harco elected to abandon the product line a=
nd direct company resources to more promising initiatives.

You might note that our fabricated fittings are not physically differentiat=
ed.  They are pursued as an incremental effort either necessary to support =
the sale of a product line that is differentiated or as an opportunity for =
incremental gross profit albeit at a lower margin.  These products could be=
 thought of as a service offering rather than a physical product.  We are s=
elling fabrication services sort of like a jobbing foundry offers casting s=
ervices.  Our business could not be structured the way it is if our entire =
business was fabrication.  Fabricated fittings are less than 1/5th of our b=
usiness.

Strategy dictates the choice of effective sales tactics and policies.

J


